      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm trying to run this macro for our monthly reconciliation but i keep getting a \"run-time error '1004': application-defined or object-defined error\" on this line:\n\nCells(i, 4).Value = wsRecon.Range(\"B\" & i).Value\n\ncan u tell me why this is happening? here is the sub:\n\nSub MonthlyRecon()\n    Dim wsRecon As Worksheet\n    Dim i As Integer\n    Set wsRecon = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Recon_Data_Final\")\n    \n    For i = 2 To 500\n        Cells(i, 4).Value = wsRecon.Range(\"B\" & i).Value\n    Next i\nEnd Sub",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The \"application-defined or object-defined error\" is a generic error message, but in this case, it's likely due to the fact that you're trying to access a range on a worksheet that doesn't exist. You're referencing `wsRecon.Range(\"B\" & i)`, but you're only setting values in `Cells(i, 4)`. \n\nThis is a bit like trying to navigate a maze without a map, you're moving around, but you're not actually reaching the destination. In this case, the destination is the range `B` on the `wsRecon` worksheet. You need to make sure that the range exists before trying to access it.\n\nTo fix this, you can try using the `Offset` method to create the range on the fly. Here's an example:\n\n```vba\nSub MonthlyRecon()\n    Dim wsRecon As Worksheet\n    Dim i As Integer\n    Set wsRecon = ThisWorkbook.Sheets(\"Recon_Data_Final\")\n    \n    For i = 2 To 500\n        Cells(i, 4).Value = wsRecon.Range(\"B\" & i).Value\n    Next i\nEnd Sub\n```\n\nHowever, this will still throw",
